      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While scientifically we categorize these as internal neural noise or recalibration errors, the *subjective experience* of it feeling like a mischievous, external presence is a very real phenomenon in how the brain interprets sensory data. When the motor control system is struggling with micro-adjustments, the brain's interpretation of that persistent, tiny error signal can easily morph into a feeling of external interference. It's the difference between the objective data (a timing error) and the subjective feeling (a deliberate jab). These tiny neural misfires, when they happen repeatedly, can feel just as persistent and irritating as a prankster in the middle of a perfectly good run.",
      "source": "target",
